 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE FOUR MASTERS. Between 1632 and 1636 the friar Mícheál Ó Cléirigh and three other scholars gathered the old Irish records into the Annals of the Four Masters, a history of Ireland year by year from the legendary Flood to 1616. This obelisk remembers them.",
   "plaque": "donegal_four_masters",
   "title": "The Annals of the Four Masters"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE DIAMOND. Laid out as the market square of the plantation town in the 1600s. It is shaped more like a triangle, which is a very Donegal way to be a diamond.",
   "plaque": "donegal_diamond",
   "title": "The Diamond"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "DONEGAL CASTLE. The O'Donnells, lords of Tír Chonaill, built the tower house in the 1400s. Red Hugh O'Donnell's family partly wrecked it rather than let the English have it; Sir Basil Brooke then added the gabled manor house in the 1620s.",
   "plaque": "donegal_castle",
   "title": "Donegal Castle",
   "local": {
    "ga": "Caisleán Dhún na nGall"
   }
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "DÚN NA nGALL means 'the fort of the foreigners'. The foreigners in question were probably Vikings, who are thought to have had a fort here on the Eske more than a thousand years ago.",
   "plaque": "donegal_name",
   "title": "Dún na nGall"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "DONEGAL ABBEY. A Franciscan friary founded in 1474 by Red Hugh O'Donnell, the first of that name, and his wife Nuala. Some of the Four Masters were friars of this house, though they did their great work elsewhere.",
   "plaque": "donegal_abbey",
   "title": "Donegal Abbey",
   "local": {
    "ga": "Mainistir Dhún na nGall"
   }
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "THE COUNTY DONEGAL RAILWAYS. Narrow-gauge lines once ran more than a hundred miles through the county, with railcars bustling up the glens. The last of them closed at the end of 1959, which is why Donegal has no trains today.",
   "plaque": "donegal_railways",
   "title": "The Donegal Railways"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "DONEGAL TWEED. A tweed of the county, woven since the 1800s and famous for its flecks: tiny bright knots of colour in the yarn, like heather and gorse on a hillside. One Donegal Town firm has been selling it since 1866.",
   "plaque": "donegal_tweed_trade",
   "title": "Donegal Tweed"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "DONEGAL BAY. Out past the bay, the coast road runs west to Killybegs, the biggest fishing port in Ireland, and on to the sea cliffs of Slieve League, about 600 metres high and among the highest in Europe.",
   "plaque": "donegal_bay",
   "title": "Donegal Bay",
   "local": {
    "ga": "Bá Dhún na nGall"
   }
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "FINGERPOST. North: the Barnesmore Gap, Glenveagh and Errigal. West: Killybegs and Slieve League (by boat or bus, not today). Malin Head, Ireland's northernmost point, is a long way up the other road."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "MALIN HEAD. Ireland's most northerly point is up at the tip of Inishowen, in this county. It has a weather station, a signal tower and, in 2016, a visit from a film crew making Star Wars, who borrowed the cliffs for a galaxy far, far away.",
   "plaque": "donegal_malin_head",
   "title": "Malin Head"
  }
 ],
